---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';
import { collectPosts } from '../../lib/posts';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Single Player PS5 Games in 2026',
  description:
    'The best single player PS5 games in 2026, from story-driven epics to challenging roguelikes. Real picks with prices and who each game is for.',
  category: 'deals',
  type: 'list',
  keyword: 'best single player ps5 games',
  date: '2026-09-19',
  updated: '2026-09-19',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: false,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=astro+bot+ps5',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
};

const related = collectPosts(import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { eager: true }))
  .filter((p) => p.title !== meta.title)
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  related={related}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What is the best single player PS5 game overall?',
      a: 'Astro Bot is the safest overall pick. It won Game of the Year, showcases everything the DualSense can do, and appeals to nearly every player regardless of skill.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What are the best single player PS5 exclusives?',
      a: 'Astro Bot, God of War Ragnarok, Spider-Man 2, Returnal, Demon\'s Souls, and Final Fantasy VII Rebirth are the standout single-player exclusives you cannot play elsewhere.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Are single player PS5 games expensive in 2026?',
      a: 'New first-party games list at $59.99 to $69.99, but most 2022 to 2024 releases now sit at $39.99 to $49.99, and PS Plus Extra bundles a large back catalog for one subscription.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Should I buy discs or digital?',
      a: 'Discs can be resold and go on sale more often at retailers. Digital is more convenient and cannot be lost or scratched. If you rarely replay games, discs cost less over time.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Some of us want to play alone. No lobbies, no matchmaking, no teammate
    rage. If that sounds like you, the best single player PS5 games in 2026 are
    exactly what the console does best. Sony has spent this generation pouring
    money into single-player blockbusters, and most of the early exclusives
    have dropped to $40 or less. Rather than a ranked list of ten, here are the
    games I would hand someone based on what they actually like to play, with
    honest pricing and who each one is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Best overall: Astro Bot</h2>

  <p>
    Astro Bot is the rare game that is both a technical showcase and a genuine
    joy. It won Game of the Year in 2024 for a reason. Every level uses the
    DualSense in ways no other game does, from the adaptive triggers to the
    haptics that make walking across sand feel different from walking across
    glass. It is approachable for kids and adults alike, and it is the game I
    would buy for anyone with a new PS5. It lists at $59.99 and is worth every
    cent.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=astro+bot+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Astro Bot
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best story: God of War Ragnarok</h2>

  <p>
    Ragnarok is the closest the PS5 gets to a prestige drama. It closes out the
    Norse saga with some of the best combat and storytelling in the medium, and
    the PS5 version runs at a clean 60 frames per second. It released in 2022,
    so you can now find it around $40, and it is a steal at that price. If you
    skipped it or only played the PS4 version, the upgrade is worth it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=god+of+war+ragnarok+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on God of War Ragnarok
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best open world: Marvel's Spider-Man 2</h2>

  <p>
    Spider-Man 2 gives you two heroes, a larger New York, and movement that
    still feels better than almost anything else released since. Swinging across
    the city never gets old, and the fast travel is so quick you will forget it
    exists. It is now around $49.99 and frequently drops lower during sales. If
    you want a big, satisfying blockbuster that never wastes your time, start
    here.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=spider-man+2+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Marvel's Spider-Man 2
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best RPG: Final Fantasy VII Rebirth</h2>

  <p>
    Rebirth is the ambitious middle chapter of the Final Fantasy VII remake
    trilogy. It is a huge open-world RPG with a combat system that blends
    real-time action and menu strategy, and it looks stunning on PS5. It lists
    at $69.99 but has started hitting sales around $40 to $50. If you want a
    hundred-hour RPG that justifies the console's power, this is it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=final+fantasy+7+rebirth+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Final Fantasy VII Rebirth
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best for a challenge: Returnal</h2>

  <p>
    Returnal is a roguelike shooter that uses the DualSense better than almost
    any title. The adaptive triggers change feel depending on which firing mode
    you are using, and the game is genuinely hard in a way that rewards
    learning. It is not for everyone, but if you want a skill test that shows
    off what the console can do, it is essential. It is frequently on sale
    around $40.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=returnal+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Returnal
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best remake: Demon's Souls</h2>

  <p>
    Demon's Souls is the PS5 launch game that still shows off the hardware. The
    Bluepoint remake rebuilt the 2009 classic with 4K visuals and a locked 60
    frames per second, and the loading times are basically instant. It is
    punishingly hard in the way FromSoftware games are, but it is a pure
    single-player experience with no microtransactions and no online
    requirement. It lists at $69.99 and frequently drops to $30 or less.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=demons+souls+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Demon's Souls
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Best platformer showcase: Ratchet &amp; Clank: Rift Apart</h2>

  <p>
    Rift Apart is the best demonstration of the PS5's SSD. The game hops between
    entire worlds in seconds, with dimensional rifts throwing you from one fully
    built planet to the next with no loading screen. It is a family-friendly
    platformer with a real story, and it still looks better than most games
    released since. It lists at $69.99 but is frequently on sale around $30 to
    $40.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=ratchet+and+clank+rift+apart+ps5" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on Ratchet &amp; Clank: Rift Apart
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>How to save on all of them</h2>

  <p>
    The single biggest money saver is PlayStation Plus Extra. A subscription
    bundles many of the best single player PS5 games, including older
    first-party hits, for one monthly fee. If you play several games a year,
    the subscription usually beats buying each title. Beyond that, physical
    discs drop faster than digital, so check retailer sales before you buy
    digital at full price.
  </p>

  <h2>Who should ignore this list</h2>

  <p>
    Skip it if you only play one live-service multiplayer game, because no
    single-player gem will pull you away from the game you already play every
    night. Skip it too if you own a gaming PC, since most of these titles either
    are on PC or are heading there. And if you are brand new to gaming entirely,
    buy Astro Bot and stop reading; it is the only recommendation on this list
    that matters for you.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .buy-link {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
